<Layout
  title="MTIA - Hardware - Rycode"
  description="MTIA 300, 400, 450, and 500. The 300 is in production with 216 GB HBM3E. The 400 is a 72-accelerator rack. Compute FLOPS from the 300 to the 500 is 25x."
  variant="ai"
>
  <article class="ai-model">
    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Hardware · Chips</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">MTIA</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">MTIA, short for Meta Training and Inference Accelerator, is Meta's own line of AI chips. Training is teaching a model, and inference is running it to answer requests. There are four parts: the 300, 400, 450, and 500. The purchase, including Broadcom's gigawatts, is on the <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/meta/">Meta stack</a>.</p>
    <ChipFigure src="/ai/hardware/chips/mtia.jpg" alt="Meta MTIA chip package: blue-tinted silicon and memory stacks inside a blue frame, on a black background." caption="A Meta MTIA chip package." credit="Image: Meta, via TrendForce" />
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />

    <h2 class="ai-model-section">MTIA 300</h2>
    <p>On 11 March 2026 the MTIA 300 was in production for ranking and recommendations training. Ranking and recommendation models decide which posts and ads to show people. The 300 is the first MTIA optimized for training those models. It is built from one compute chiplet and two network chiplets, where a chiplet is a small piece of silicon joined with others in one package. Each processing element, one of the chip's small compute blocks, has two RISC-V vector cores. RISC-V is an open standard for a chip's instruction set. A vector core works on many numbers at once. From the MTIA 300 to the MTIA 500, HBM bandwidth increases by 4.5 times and compute FLOPS increases by 25 times, measured from the 300's MX8 format to the 500's MX4. HBM, or high-bandwidth memory, is the fast memory stacked next to the chip. FLOPS counts math operations per second. MX8 and MX4 are number formats that store each value in 8 and 4 bits.</p>
